---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import Showcase from "../../components/Showcase.astro";
import StylingHooks from "../../components/StylingHooks.astro";

const import_ = `@import "@skryensya/core/patterns/anchored.css";`;

const anatomy = `<!-- The ANCHOR: whatever it is you measure against. Almost always the trigger. -->
<button class="sk-button sk-interactive sk-anchor" data-sk-anchor-trigger>
  Save
</button>

<!-- The POSITIONER: only placement, never painting, never clicking. -->
<span class="sk-tooltip__positioner sk-anchored" data-sk-placement="block-start">
  <!-- The ARROW, optional. Inside, but he places himself against the ANCHOR. -->
  <span class="sk-anchored-arrow" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  <!-- The CONTENT: what you see. -->
  <span class="sk-tooltip__content" data-sk-anchor-content>
    Saves only every 30 seconds
  </span>
</span>`;

const html = `<div class="sk-anclaje-demo">
  <span class="sk-tooltip" data-sk-anchor data-sk-placement="block-start">
    <button class="sk-button sk-interactive sk-tooltip__trigger sk-anchor" data-sk-anchor-trigger type="button">
      block-start
    </button>
    <span class="sk-tooltip__positioner sk-anchored" data-sk-anchor-positioner>
      <span class="sk-anchored-arrow" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <span class="sk-tooltip__content" data-sk-anchor-content>comes out from above</span>
    </span>
  </span>

  <span class="sk-tooltip" data-sk-anchor data-sk-placement="block-end">
    <button class="sk-button sk-interactive sk-tooltip__trigger sk-anchor" data-sk-anchor-trigger type="button">
      block-end
    </button>
    <span class="sk-tooltip__positioner sk-anchored" data-sk-anchor-positioner>
      <span class="sk-anchored-arrow" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <span class="sk-tooltip__content" data-sk-anchor-content>It comes out from below</span>
    </span>
  </span>

  <span class="sk-tooltip" data-sk-anchor data-sk-placement="inline-end">
    <button class="sk-button sk-interactive sk-tooltip__trigger sk-anchor" data-sk-anchor-trigger type="button">
      inline-end
    </button>
    <span class="sk-tooltip__positioner sk-anchored" data-sk-anchor-positioner>
      <span class="sk-anchored-arrow" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <span class="sk-tooltip__content" data-sk-anchor-content>Comes out to the side</span>
    </span>
  </span>

  <span class="sk-tooltip" data-sk-anchor>
    <button class="sk-button sk-interactive sk-tooltip__trigger sk-anchor" data-sk-anchor-trigger type="button">
      no arrow
    </button>
    <span class="sk-tooltip__positioner sk-anchored" data-sk-anchor-positioner>
      <span class="sk-tooltip__content" data-sk-anchor-content>The arrow is optional</span>
    </span>
  </span>
</div>`;

const js = `import { initComponents } from "@skryensya/vanilla/auto";

initComponents();`;

const escapeHatch = `/* A submenu appears on the side and aligned above: it is not any of the four.
   It is requested by the hook, not by adding a fifth to the public vocabulary. */
.sk-menu__positioner[data-sk-submenu] {
  --sk-anchored-position-area: inline-end span-block-end;
  --sk-anchored-justify: auto;
  --sk-anchored-align: start;
  --sk-anchored-position-try: flip-inline;
}`;

const sameWidth = `/* The listbox measures what its trigger measures. Goes inside the @supports:
   anchor-size() without a resolved anchor is invalid. */
@supports (anchor-name: --a) {
  .sk-select__positioner {
    --sk-anchored-size: anchor-size(width);
  }
}`;

const arrow = `<span class="sk-tooltip__positioner sk-anchored" data-sk-placement="block-start">
  <span class="sk-anchored-arrow" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  <span class="sk-tooltip__content">…</span>
</span>`;

const arrowReact = `<Tooltip arrow content="It keeps itself">
  <button className="sk-button sk-interactive">Save</button>
</Tooltip>

<Popover arrow trigger="Ver perfil" title="Ada Lovelace">
  Mathematician and writer.
</Popover>`;
---

<Base
  title="Anclaje"
  description="A pattern: placing a box against another element, with the browser as the engine and the machine as the fallback."
>
  <h1>Anclaje</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A box placed against <strong>another element</strong>, not against the page: it comes out from one side of the
    anchor, goes to the opposite side when it does not enter, and disappears if the anchor leaves the screen. It is what
    that make tooltip, popover, popup, menu and its submenus, select, combobox, date picker and flyout.
  </p>

  <Showcase html={html} js={js} label="HTML" />

  <h2>Why is it a pattern and not a component?</h2>
  <p>
    The rule is a single question: <em>could a second component need this structure
    exact?</em> Here we do not have to predict anything, <strong>nine already need it</strong>. So Anchor
    sends <strong>hooks and structure</strong>, such as <a href="/en/vaul">Vaul</a>, and not just variables.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is not confused with Vaul, although both talk about anchoring: a Vaul anchors to a <strong>edge of the
    viewport</strong>, and this is anchored to <strong>another element</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2>The three parts</h2>
  <p>
    Two flat classes that are composed <strong>compose</strong> with the component parts, such as
    <code>sk-interactive</code> with <code>sk-button</code>, plus an optional third. They are not a BEM pair
    because the anchor is not inside the positioner, and in React the positioner is ported to the
    <code>&lt;body&gt;</code>: Nesting one under the other would name a containment that does not exist.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={anatomy} label="HTML" />
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>sk-anchor</code>, the <strong>anchor</strong>. Whatever it is measured against. almost always
      the trigger, never automatically: anchoring is a geometric role and firing is an interactive one.
      Combobox and date picker anchor the <em>integer</em>control, because the list aligns with the field
      that is being written, not with the 32px chevron next to it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>sk-anchored</code>, the <strong>positioner</strong>. It takes the placement and nothing more: no
      paints and does not intercept the pointer, so the invisible box it reserves cannot block what
      there is below. Your child, the content, is what is seen and what is clicked.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>sk-anchored-arrow</code>, the <strong>arrow</strong>, optional. It is authored or it does not exist. Goes
      inside the positioner, and yet it is placed against the <strong>anchor</strong>, not against the box.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The one that places it is the browser</h2>
  <p>
    Where is the <em>CSS Anchor Positioning</em> API, place the browser: no layout loop, no
    measure on each scroll, with flipping and missing anchor resolved within the layout engine, which is
    where that information already lives. Where it is not, position the Zag machine.
  </p>
  <p>
    The fallback <strong>is not a lower path</strong>, it is the same contract executed by another
    engine. What it does require is that both do not run at the same time: in the browser route the binding
    takes out the <code>style</code> inline that the machine comes with to the positioner, and that is the reason why
    pattern declarations can run out of <code>!important</code>. It is structural load, not
    verbose: <code>position-try</code> can only flip statements that <strong>not</strong> are
    <code>!important</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Four placements, on logical axes</h2>
  <p>
    <code>block-start</code>, <code>block-end</code>, <code>inline-start</code> and
    <code>inline-end</code>, orders with <code>data-sk-placement</code> on the
    <strong>positioner</strong> and not on the root, because in React the positioner is ported and the
    inheritance from root does not get there. They turn around on their own in RTL.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is a <strong>preference, not a guarantee</strong>: if it does not fit, the browser flips it to the side
    opposite of the <em>same axis</em>. Whoever orders <code>inline-end</code> wants the box on the side, and
    To fall above would be to disobey, not adapt.
  </p>

  <h2>Rare cases use the hook, not a fifth placement</h2>
  <p>
    The vocabulary is not enlarged by exceptions. A submenu wants to appear on the side aligned by
    above, and that is requested by overriding the hook directly, which is what a hook is for. Let the
    exception write the contract is how a set of four becomes a set of nine.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={escapeHatch} label="CSS" />
  <p>
    The other hook that is really used is the width. Select and Combobox measure what your anchor measures:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={sameWidth} label="CSS" />

  <h2>The arrow is optional, and is authored</h2>
  <p>
    A square rotated 45° that peeks over the edge of the box towards the anchor. If the markup does not have
    <code>.sk-anchored-arrow</code>, there is no arrow, and that is the default. A
    <a href="/en/components/tooltip">tooltip</a> or a <a href="/en/components/popover">popover</a> the
    They want because they are floating chrome that <em> has to tell what control</em> is talking about; a
    menu or a select no, because there the relationship is already stated by the shared border.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={arrow} label="HTML" />
  <p>In React it is a prop, turned off by default:</p>
  <CodeBlock code={arrowReact} label="React" />
  <p>
    It is <strong>decorative</strong>, so it always goes with <code>aria-hidden</code>: it does not add
    information, repeats what the placement already gives.
  </p>

  <h3>It comes from the anchor, not from the center of the box</h3>
  <p>
    The difference is seen as soon as the box is moved: <code>anchor-center</code> centers it on the anchor
    <em>but</em> puts it back on screen if it does not go in, so a trigger near the edge leaves the
    displaced box, and an arrow drawn 50% of that box ends up pointing anywhere but
    to control. So the arrow is not placed against the box: it is <strong>another anchored box</strong>
    against the same anchor, with the same <code>position-area</code> and the same turn.
  </p>
  <p>
    And yet <strong>lives inside</strong> of the positioner, which would seem impossible: the spec only leaves
    use as anchor something that is <em>descendant of the containing block</em> of the element, and the trigger
    it does not hang from the positioner. What solves it is <code>position: fixed</code>: the containing block
    of a fixed one is the <strong>viewport</strong>, where the trigger does live, although in the DOM the arrow
    continue inside. With <code>absolute</code> the containing block would be the positioner and
    <code>anchor()</code> and <code>anchor-center</code> would become invalid <em>silently</em>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Staying inside is not prolixity either, it is necessary for three things at the same time. The box of a
    <a href="/en/components/popover">Popover</a> goes to the <em>top layer</em> when it opens, and only enters there
    with his children. As a positioned daughter, the arrow paints <strong>above</strong> the background and border
    of the box, which is what makes the seam disappear. And in the <strong>fallback</strong> place the
    machine, which looks for the arrow inside the floating element.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Flips together with the box</em> because it takes the height of the box as a margin on the outside
    (<code>anchor-size()</code>): they both have the same footprint, so they cross the threshold of
    <code>position-try</code> at the same time.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Remaining cost:</strong> when flipping, <code>position-try</code> flips margins and
    alignment but not the <code>rotate</code>, which is not a property it accepts. The rhombus is symmetrical
    at 180°, so the shape does not change and only which of its sides carry the border change: invisible
    with the borderless arrow of a tooltip, a line on the side that is not when the border is painted.
  </p>

  <h2>Who uses it</h2>
  <p>
    Seven components make up <code>sk-anchored</code> today. The last column says who solves the
    placement: almost all of them stay on the browser path, and the only one that also has positioned
    per machine is the tooltip.
  </p>
  <div class="sk-table-scroll">
    <table class="sk-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Component</th>
          <th scope="col">What an anchor</th>
          <th scope="col">Flecha</th>
          <th scope="col">Coloca</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><a href="/en/components/tooltip">Tooltip</a></td>
          <td>the trigger</td>
          <td>yes</td>
          <td>browser + machine (Zag)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><a href="/en/components/popover">Popover</a></td>
          <td>the trigger</td>
          <td>yes</td>
          <td>navegador</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><a href="/en/components/popup">Popup</a></td>
          <td>the trigger</td>
          <td>yes, opt-in</td>
          <td>navegador</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><a href="/en/components/menu">Menu</a> and its submenus</td>
          <td>the trigger; the parent item in the submenu</td>
          <td>no</td>
          <td>navegador</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><a href="/en/components/select">Select</a></td>
          <td>the entire control</td>
          <td>no</td>
          <td>navegador</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><a href="/en/components/combobox">Combobox</a></td>
          <td>the entire control</td>
          <td>no</td>
          <td>navegador</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><a href="/en/components/date-picker">DatePicker</a></td>
          <td>the entire control</td>
          <td>no</td>
          <td>navegador</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The two cuts in the table are the same argument from above, already applied. The arrow is brought by the
    three that are floating <strong>chrome</strong> and have to say what control they are talking about; menu,
    select and combobox do not, because there the border shared with the anchor already says the relationship. and those who
    anchor the <em>integercontrol</em> are the ones that are written inside: the list is aligned with the field,
    not with the 32px chevron next to it.
  </p>

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <p>
    It is opt-in, like all patterns: it is imported before the components that consume it, because
    They are your consumers.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={import_} label="CSS" />

  <StylingHooks groups={[{ file: "patterns/anchored.css", probe: "sk-anchored" }]} />
</Base>

<style is:global>
  /* La piel del demo es del consumidor, como siempre: el pattern trae hooks y estructura, no pintura.
     `is:global`: el HTML autorado vive dentro del srcdoc y no lleva el atributo de scope de Astro. */
  .sk-anclaje-demo {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-inline-md);
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    /* Aire arriba y abajo para que un tooltip block-start tenga a dónde ir sin que el navegador lo
       tenga que voltear: el demo enseña la colocación pedida, no el fallback. */
    padding-block: var(--space-stack-xl);
  }
</style>
